{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CfdnHKjZqJfZhxjCENGEz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tests green. Here's how I handled each finding:\n\n**Finding 1 — inconsistent import style. Fixed.** Added `import java.util.List;` and changed the inline `java.util.List.of(...)` to `List.of(\"telephone\")`, so the handler now matches the clean import block of its sibling `MissingOwnerFieldsExceptionHandler`.\n\n**Finding 2 — exception message unused by handler. Deliberate choice to leave as-is.** I agree it's worth a conscious decision rather than an accident, so here it is: unlike `MissingOwnerFieldsException`, whose `getFields()` carries a *variable* set of offending field names that genuinely must flow to the response, the invalid-telephone case has an *invariant* offending field — it is always `\"telephone\"`. So there's no per-instance data to propagate; hardcoding the field in the handler is correct and clearer than deriving it. The exception message (the digit count) remains a legitimate diagnostic for logs/escalation traces, which is why I kept it rather than dropping it. Making the handler parse that human-readable message back into a field name would be strictly worse. So the two are intentionally decoupled, not accidentally mismatched.\n\nFull suite: `BUILD SUCCESS`, 2 tests, 0 failures — acceptance behaviour unchanged (`0412345678` returned on normalize, 400 on too-few digits)."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":614,"cache_read_input_tokens":41449,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":614},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1b78f45d-8717-41c8-91c5-349804465903","uuid":"3c3407e9-9424-4ce4-bb1f-e200ab8fd21